    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashikaga-takauji">Ashikaga Takauji</a> set up his military government in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a> in 1336 after overthrowing the Kenmu Restoration of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-go-daigo">Emperor Go-Daigo</a>.</li><li>It was the weakest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>'s three military governments, depending on the loyalty of the daimyō because the shōgun held little territory of his own.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobunaga">Oda Nobunaga</a> drove the fifteenth shōgun, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashikaga-yoshiaki">Ashikaga Yoshiaki</a>, out of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a> in 1573, ending the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The name Muromachi comes from the Muromachi district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a>, where the third shōgun, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashikaga-yoshimitsu">Ashikaga Yoshimitsu</a>, built his residence known as the &quot;Flower Palace&quot; in 1379.</p></aside>
    </section>

