    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>From 1180 to 1185 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genpei-war">Genpei War</a> between the Taira and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minamoto-clan">Minamoto clans</a> ended with the victory of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minamoto-no-yoritomo">Minamoto no Yoritomo</a> and the founding of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamakura">Kamakura</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a>, later dominated by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hojo-clan">Hōjō clan</a>. The Hōjō monopoly of power, and the lack of a reward of lands after the defeat of the Mongol invasions, left their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> resentful. In 1333 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-go-daigo">Emperor Go-Daigo</a> called on local vassals to oppose Hōjō rule in the Kenmu Restoration. Sent by Kamakura to crush the rising, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashikaga-takauji">Ashikaga Takauji</a> turned against the shogunate for reasons that remain unclear and helped overthrow it.</p><p>Go-Daigo's policies were unpopular and failed to satisfy those who had fought for him. In 1336 Takauji set up his own military government in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a> and made himself shōgun. A dispute with Go-Daigo over how to govern led Takauji to have Prince Yutahito installed as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-komyo">Emperor Kōmyō</a>, while Go-Daigo fled the capital. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> was divided between a Northern Court in Kyoto under Ashikaga influence and a Southern Court in Yoshino, the beginning of the Nanboku-chō period.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a> gave more attention to the arts and to trade than to gaining control of surrounding territories. It traded with Joseon <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>, using the daimyō of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsushima-island">Tsushima Island</a> as middlemen, and with imperial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, where goods were exchanged and labelled as offerings from one emperor to another. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashikaga-yoshimitsu">Ashikaga Yoshimitsu</a> expanded these relations, receiving large quantities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porcelain">porcelain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> and bronze coins from China and sending swords, lumber and ores in return. Marginal trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a> sent similar goods, together with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">furs</a>, for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spice-trade">spices</a> and other kinds of lumber.</p><p>The economic base of the old order shifted to the provinces. The shugo appointed by the shogunate absorbed the judicial, economic and taxation powers of the local Imperial governors, and government holdings in each province passed rapidly into the personal holdings of the daimyō and their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a>. The Imperial court lost both its political weight and its economic base.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>For 56 years the Northern and Southern Courts struggled for power. The Northern Court usually dominated, but the Southern Court was occasionally able to capture the capital; each time it was pushed back by the larger Northern armies. The struggle ended in 1392, under the third shōgun, when the Southern Court gave up and the two courts were combined.</p><p>Because Takauji had little personal territory, the military power of the shōgun depended on the loyalty of the daimyō, and the regime was weak militarily. Bloody succession crises among the warrior families wore down its authority, and the daimyō feuded among themselves for power. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/onin-war">Ōnin War</a> of 1467 to 1477 turned these feuds into open warfare and began the constant civil war of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sengoku-period">Sengoku period</a>. In 1565 the shōgun <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashikaga-yoshiteru">Ashikaga Yoshiteru</a> was assassinated, and the daimyō <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobunaga">Oda Nobunaga</a> used the opening to install Yoshiteru's brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashikaga-yoshiaki">Ashikaga Yoshiaki</a> as shōgun.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Ashikaga <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a> is described as the weakest of the three Japanese military governments. Unlike the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamakura">Kamakura</a> shogunate before it or the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa shogunate</a> after it, it relied heavily on the prestige and personal authority of the shōgun. The centralised master-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> system of Kamakura gave way to a highly decentralised system of daimyō, local lords whose authority grew far beyond that of Kamakura times.</p><p>The Imperial court was no longer a credible threat. The failure of the Kenmu Restoration had left it weak and subservient, and Takauji reinforced this by placing his government near the Emperor in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a>. Powers lost by the court passed to the shōgun, and this position reached its peak under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashikaga-yoshimitsu">Ashikaga Yoshimitsu</a>. After him the structural weakness of the regime was exposed by repeated succession crises and early deaths. The list of shōgun runs to fifteen, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashikaga-takauji">Ashikaga Takauji</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashikaga-yoshiaki">Ashikaga Yoshiaki</a>, with gaps and a second reign for Ashikaga Yoshitane.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Ashikaga era was a period of great development in Japanese culture and art, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zen">Zen Buddhism</a> as a large contributor. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japanese-tea-ceremony">tea ceremony</a> moved from a large open room to a small, quiet setting and became a form of meditation. Flower arranging became an art of spiritual value, its arrangements often used as offerings to the Buddha. Noh <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> was created in this period; its performances were enjoyed and protected by nobles and elites. They helped to widen the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theme-system">themes</a> of shows and to produce poetry. The performing arts suffered during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/onin-war">Ōnin War</a> but recovered once power had fully shifted.</p><p>Abroad, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a>'s contacts with Joseon and with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> shaped its foreign relations, and its trade carried Japanese swords, lumber and ores across the sea. The name of the Muromachi district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a>, where Yoshimitsu built his residence, gave the period its name.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The authority of the bakufu had almost vanished by 1441, at the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashikaga-yoshinori">Ashikaga Yoshinori</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/onin-war">Ōnin War</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a> was reduced to little more than a local political force in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a>. The puppet shōgun <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashikaga-yoshiaki">Ashikaga Yoshiaki</a>, installed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobunaga">Oda Nobunaga</a> in 1568, was not wholly subservient: he struck bargains with the monasteries and mediated between powerful clans such as the Otomo and Mori. In 1573 Nobunaga drove him out of Kyoto, and the shogunate was destroyed. Yoshiaki fled first to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shikoku">Shikoku</a> and later received the protection of the Mōri clan in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>.</p><p>The Ashikaga family survived the 16th century, and a branch of it became the daimyō family of the Kitsuregawa domain. The site of the "Flower Palace" in Kyoto, bounded by Karasuma, Imadegawa, Muromachi and Kamidachiuri streets, is marked by a stone, and relics and excavations of the area are kept in the Kanbai-kan of Dōshisha University.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Ashikaga <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a> ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a> between 1336 and 1573, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashikaga-takauji">Ashikaga Takauji</a> overthrew the Kenmu Restoration. Weak in land and arms, it leaned on the daimyō, reached its height under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashikaga-yoshimitsu">Ashikaga Yoshimitsu</a>, encouraged Zen arts and Noh, and dissolved into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sengoku-period">Sengoku</a> wars until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobunaga">Oda Nobunaga</a> ended it.</em></p>

